<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bernt Notke</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1440–<a href="/years/1509">1509</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bernt Notke was a painter and sculptor based in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a>, the leading artist of the Baltic region in the late 15th century. His works include the Danse Macabre now in St Nicholas' Church in Tallinn (Reval), the high altar of Tallinn's Church of the Holy Spirit and the monumental Saint George and the Dragon in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm">Stockholm</a> Cathedral.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bernt-notke.jpg" alt="Bernt Notke" width="122" height="174" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bernt Notke · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bernt_Notke_goss_74a.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Very little is known of Notke's life. He was born in the small town of Lassan in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a> into a family from Tallinn; his father was probably the trader and ship-owner Michel Notke, his mother probably Michel's second wife Gertraut, from Visby. He seems to have spent part of his youth in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>, probably in the workshop of the tapestry weaver Pasquier Grenier in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tournai">Tournai</a>, where he learned to work on a large scale and to divide the labour of a workshop.</p><p>In the early 1460s he settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a>, where he first appears in the records of the city council on 14 April 1467, called a painter. In 1479 he bought a stone house on Breite Strasse. He spent 1491 to 1497 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm">Stockholm</a>, holding for three years the office of mint master of the realm, and left after the end of the regency of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sten-sture-the-elder">Sten Sture the Elder</a>. In 1505 he became Werkmeister of the Church of Saint Peter in Lübeck.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Notke headed a workshop that made tapestries, wooden sculptures and paintings, above all altarpieces combining sculpture and painting. Whether he was chiefly a painter, a woodworker or an organiser is unclear. His first known work, made between 1463 and 1466, was a Danse Macabre at least 26 metres long for St. Mary's Church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a>, lost in the bombing of 1942 and known from a copy of 1701 by Anton Wortmann.</p><p>For Lübeck Cathedral he made in 1470–1478 a triumphal cross of 72 oak sculptures, commissioned by Bishop Albert Krummedik. The altarpiece of Aarhus Cathedral, inaugurated in 1479, was at 12 metres the largest in the Nordic countries. The high altar of the Church of the Holy Ghost in Tallinn, finished in 1483, was the first in the Baltic region to show a biblical scene in its central panel rather than a line of saints. The Saint George and the Dragon in Storkyrkan was commissioned by Sten Sture to mark his victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christian-i-of-denmark">Christian I of Denmark</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-brunkeberg">Battle of Brunkeberg</a> in 1471.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Notke died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> in 1509; his wife had died before him, and they had two daughters, one named Anneke. Philippe Dollinger wrote that if any artist can be called Hanseatic, it is Notke, though others have called him a routine producer of altarpieces. His influence reached as far south as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneburg">Lüneburg</a>, and his pupils included Heinrich Wylsynck and Henning van der Heide, the most accomplished of his followers.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bernt Notke” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Lübeck artist <b>Bernt Notke</b> painted the Danse Macabre now displayed in St Nicholas' Church and the altarpiece of the Church of the Holy Spirit, while Hermen Rode's high altar survives in St Nicholas.</blockquote><a href="/empire/reval">Reval · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Lassan, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.93,13.83&amp;year=1484" aria-label="Lassan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Bernt Notke"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5690 5445h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5690 5445h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=53.93,13.83&amp;year=1484">What was here in 1484? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
